  "body": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Arepas are made from a simple "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "masa"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "masa"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " of fine polenta, water, and salt. The dough comes together in minutes and the cakes cook on a dry "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "griddle-cooking"
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "griddle"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " until crisp on the outside and soft in the middle. They are split while still warm and filled with cheese that melts from the residual heat."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Tip: the dough should feel like soft play-dough. If it cracks at the edges when you press it flat, add water a tablespoon at a time."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "What you need"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "ingredientsList",
        "attrs": {
          "defaultServings": 4,
          "items": [
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "polenta",
              "amount": 300,
              "unit": "g",
              "prepNote": "fine-ground, or masarepa corn flour",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "sea-salt-fine",
              "amount": 1,
              "unit": "tsp",
              "prepNote": null,
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "whole-milk",
              "amount": 100,
              "unit": "ml",
              "prepNote": "optional, for richness",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "unsalted-butter",
              "amount": 20,
              "unit": "g",
              "prepNote": "softened",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            },
            {
              "ingredientSlug": "cheddar",
              "amount": 200,
              "unit": "g",
              "prepNote": "grated, or mozzarella",
              "isOptional": false,
              "groupLabel": null
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix polenta and salt in a bowl. Add butter and milk. Pour in 280ml warm water gradually, mixing with your hands until a soft, smooth dough forms."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Rest the dough for 5 minutes. This allows the polenta to hydrate fully."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Divide into 8 equal balls. Press each into a round disc about 1.5cm thick. Smooth any cracks at the edges with wet fingers."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat a dry griddle pan over medium heat. Cook arepas for 6 to 7 minutes per side until a firm golden crust forms. They should sound slightly hollow when tapped."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Remove from the heat. While still hot, use a knife to split each arepa open like a pocket. Fill with grated cheese and close. The residual heat will melt it."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Serve immediately while warm."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Arepas crack badly when pressed",
              "cause": "The dough is too dry.",
              "fix": "Add water one tablespoon at a time and knead briefly. The dough should press flat without cracking at the edges."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Crust is very pale after 7 minutes",
              "cause": "The griddle was not hot enough.",
              "fix": "Increase the heat slightly. The pan should feel very hot when you hold your hand 10cm above it."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Cheese does not melt inside",
              "cause": "The arepa cooled before filling.",
              "fix": "Fill the arepa within 1 minute of leaving the griddle. If needed, return the filled arepa to the pan for 30 seconds per side."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Arepas are eaten every day across Colombia and Venezuela. They predate the arrival of Europeans by thousands of years and are one of the oldest foods of the Americas. Each region of Colombia makes arepas slightly differently. On the Caribbean coast they are thin and crisp. In Antioquia they are thick and served with butter. In Bogota they come stuffed with cheese and egg. The con queso version is the most universal, sold from carts and eaten at breakfast, as a school snack, and late at night."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}